I have a few suggestions to improve the benchmark:

1. Let's split the benchmark into separate operations - insertion and removal. This will give us more granular performance insights for each operation.
2. Please use `b.Run()` calls to create sub-benchmarks for different data sizes and operations.
3. Call `b.ResetTimer()` to ensure we're only timing the operations we intend to measure, not the setup code. Once the setup is complete (initializing queue, dataset, channel, etc.) make a call to `b.ResetTimer()` to ensure these operations don't affect the benchmark results.

You can probably structure it this way:

```go
func BenchmarkQueueRef(b *testing.B) {
    benchmarkCases := []struct {
        name         string
        expiredFirst int
        nonExpired   int
        expiredLast  int
    }{
        {"Small", 10, 20, 30},
        {"Medium", 100, 200, 300},
        {"Large", 100, 2000, 3000},
        {"VeryLarge", 10000, 20000, 30000},
    }

    for _, bc := range benchmarkCases {
        // Setup code...

        b.Run(fmt.Sprintf("%s_Insert", bc.name), func(b *testing.B) {
            for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
                // Insertion benchmark...
            }
        })

        b.Run(fmt.Sprintf("%s_Remove", bc.name), func(b *testing.B) {
            for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
                // Removal benchmark...
            }
        })
    }
}

